1
00:00:00,348 --> 00:00:03,594
We introduce GEM, a geometric episodic memory

2
00:00:03,594 --> 00:00:05,600
that uses a streaming geometry model

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,512
to help existing navigation policies

4
00:00:07,512 --> 00:00:10,850
return to previously observed places without retraining.

5
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:15,441
We first show how this memory supports an indoor revisit,

6
00:00:15,441 --> 00:00:19,218
where the robot must return to a place shown in a goal image.

7
00:00:19,218 --> 00:00:21,678
Both runs share the same starting pose,

8
00:00:21,678 --> 00:00:24,082
goal image, and survey history,

9
00:00:24,082 --> 00:00:26,773
but the baseline stops before a glass wall,

10
00:00:26,773 --> 00:00:30,454
while GEM uses that history to reach the destination.

11
00:00:32,452 --> 00:00:36,215
In the outdoor trial, GEM turns toward the remembered goal

12
00:00:36,215 --> 00:00:39,128
and updates its direction as the robot moves,

13
00:00:39,128 --> 00:00:43,215
while the unchanged NavDP controller plans the local motion.

14
00:00:43,215 --> 00:00:45,465
Across ten real-world settings,

15
00:00:45,465 --> 00:00:48,728
GEM reaches the goal in 25/30 trials,

16
00:00:48,728 --> 00:00:51,412
compared with 4/30 for the baseline.

17
00:00:56,287 --> 00:01:00,850
We also evaluate in Habitat on HM3D and MP3D.

18
00:01:01,465 --> 00:01:05,678
In this episode, both methods first reach two novel goals,

19
00:01:05,678 --> 00:01:08,028
collecting observations that remain available

20
00:01:08,028 --> 00:01:11,928
when the third goal asks them to revisit an earlier location.

21
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:13,890
GEM uses this accumulated history

22
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:16,752
to locate the goal relative to the current camera,

23
00:01:16,752 --> 00:01:20,188
allowing it to complete the sequence while the baseline fails.

24
00:01:22,314 --> 00:01:25,156
These revisits rely on shared geometry,

25
00:01:25,156 --> 00:01:28,369
which a frozen model builds by estimating pose and depth

26
00:01:28,369 --> 00:01:32,000
from RGB images, without external pose measurements.

27
00:01:32,447 --> 00:01:35,482
This memory combines a working state for streaming geometry

28
00:01:35,482 --> 00:01:38,739
with an archive that links past images to their geometry,

29
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:42,100
and both are retained as the robot moves between goals.

30
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:44,402
When a new goal image is given,

31
00:01:44,402 --> 00:01:46,978
GEM matches it to a supporting historical view

32
00:01:46,978 --> 00:01:51,352
and uses archived depth to estimate and verify the goal pose.

33
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:53,840
The shared geometry then connects that pose

34
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:57,450
to the current camera, even without direct image overlap.

35
00:01:59,397 --> 00:02:01,256
Once the goal pose is cached,

36
00:02:01,256 --> 00:02:04,232
GEM updates its bearing as the robot moves.

37
00:02:04,232 --> 00:02:08,032
NavDP receives this cue alongside the original goal image,

38
00:02:08,032 --> 00:02:11,432
while ViNT and NoMaD use the verified historical view;

39
00:02:11,432 --> 00:02:14,700
if verification fails, the original inputs are retained.

40
00:02:15,342 --> 00:02:17,513
To test these benefits across controllers,

41
00:02:17,513 --> 00:02:21,506
we compare three frozen policies on HM3D and MP3D

42
00:02:21,506 --> 00:02:24,807
under matched histories and evaluation conditions.

43
00:02:24,807 --> 00:02:27,110
Adding GEM improves revisit success

44
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:29,984
by 60.7 to 81.0 percentage points,

45
00:02:29,984 --> 00:02:32,857
while success on novel goals is maintained or improved

46
00:02:32,857 --> 00:02:34,700
across all six comparisons.

47
00:02:35,287 --> 00:02:38,564
When we extend the evaluation to continuous navigation,

48
00:02:38,564 --> 00:02:41,709
where each method builds its own history across goals,

49
00:02:41,709 --> 00:02:44,711
GEM completes 64 three-goal sequences,

50
00:02:44,711 --> 00:02:46,700
compared with 12 for the baseline.

51
00:02:47,249 --> 00:02:50,568
When both methods receive the same two-goal histories,

52
00:02:50,568 --> 00:02:52,275
third-goal revisit success rises

53
00:02:52,275 --> 00:02:54,319
from eight to seventeen out of twenty,

54
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:57,550
while novel success remains 4/20 for both.
